 - `HookApi.entry()` and `entries()`, and `TaskRuntime.entries()`: a hook or task reads the conversation's raw history, which compaction never changes, so a range bounded by entry IDs reads the same before and after a compaction and on a rerun ([#11141](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/issues/11141))
 - Run-end tasks: a `GenerationHooks.afterRun(run, api)` hook returns `{ tasks }`, built with the typed `runEndTask(task, input)`, after a run answered (a final answer or a `terminate` round; not when an `onYield` continuation is taken, not after a failure or an abort). The answer is committed and the run's inputs settle `done` first, so `Submission.wait()` resolves at the answer; the generation then creates the tasks as its children and waits for them, keeping `pi.live.run` (with `run.afterRun`) and the conversation busy, so idle waits include them, Stop aborts them, and a crash resumes them. Input queued meanwhile, steers included, is placed when they are terminal and starts the next run; a task admits a model-visible write or a fix-run follow-up in its own commit with `submit(tx, draft)`. A crash before the tasks exist asks the hooks again; a broken request (an uninstalled or built-in task, a non-JSON input, a throwing `initial()`) is reported and skipped. `run` names the run's entries: `first` (`pi.live.run.first`, carried into `runFailure.first` so a continue covers a failed run's history), `answer`, and `last` ([#11141](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/issues/11141))
 
+- `GenerationHooks.beforeRetry(failed, api)`: a host decides whether and when a failed request attempt is tried again, instead of `settings.retry`. It is asked for a failed message (`reason: "model_error"`, with the message) and for a request or poll the host stopped during (`interrupted`), with the attempt, the error text, and the policy's own decision (`delayMs`, absent when the policy would end the run). The first decision wins: `{ retry: true, delayMs? }` retries after `delayMs` (default the policy's backoff), also past `maxRetries` or with the policy disabled; `{ retry: false }` ends the run with the failure; no decision leaves it to the policy. Not asked for an overflow that starts a compaction. New types `FailedAttempt` and `RetryDecision`
+
